TITLE:: DrumProgrammer
SUMMARY:: Drum-machine step grid with looping playback.
CATEGORIES:: GUI>Views
RELATED:: Classes/AmpCommand, Classes/CommandPrompt, Classes/DrumStep, Classes/EnhancedButton, Classes/GridSlider, Classes/GuiPalette, Classes/PlayableNote, Classes/RealTimePlayer, Classes/SCViewHolder, Classes/ScrollView, Classes/Sequencer, Classes/SequencerGui, Classes/StaticText, Classes/View, Classes/Window

DESCRIPTION::
TELETYPE::DrumProgrammer:: is a LINK::Classes/SCViewHolder:: that shows a grid of step squares on a LINK::Classes/GuiPalette:: colour1 background. Sound names and MIDI note numbers are held in a class LINK::Classes/Dictionary:: for now. It opens with one row of 16 squares for each sound, ordered by MIDI note number ascending from top to bottom: Kick 2 (35), Kick 1 (36), Rim (37), Snare (38), Clap (39), Tom 1 (41), Closed hi-hat (42), Tom 2 (43), Open hi-hat (46), Cymbal (49), Ride (51), Cowbell (56). Each row has its sound name in a LINK::Classes/StaticText:: on the left. That label column is as wide as the longest name. Click a sound name to select that row; click it again to clear the selection, or click another name to move it. While the mouse is held on that name, the label background changes to colour3, and a note for that sound is sent on channel 10 at velocity 100 for the same length as a grid hit. The background returns to colour1 when the button is released or the pointer leaves the label, and it changes back to colour3 if the pointer re-enters while the button is still down. A name with no MIDI note number still selects the row and still changes colour, but sends no note. The selected row's squares are outlined with LINK::Classes/GuiPalette#-extreme2::, unless that square is also in the column currently playing. A LINK::Classes/CommandPrompt:: along the top offers LINK::Classes/AmpCommand::. The selected row's LINK::Classes/DrumStep::s, including empty squares, are that command's selected notes. CODE::amp 1 0 0 0:: paints hit, rest, rest, rest, and then repeats. A value above CODE::0:: turns an empty square into a hit. CODE::amp 0:: clears a hit. The grid sits in a LINK::Classes/ScrollView:: above the button strip, which stays put, and a scrollbar appears only when the grid is larger than that area.

Each square is a LINK::Classes/GridSlider:: held by a LINK::Classes/DrumStep::. The empty colour is colour5 and the hit colour is colour3, growing up from the bottom. The top of the square is amplitude CODE::1:: and the bottom pixel is CODE::0::. Left click or left drag turns the hit on and sets the amplitude at the pointer. Dragging back above CODE::0:: in the same gesture turns the hit on again. Dragging to CODE::0::, or a right click, turns the hit off. Each hit is a LINK::Classes/PlayableNote::. The column is a sixteenth note (start time CODE::columnIndex * 0.25:: beats) and the note lasts CODE::0.0625:: beats. MIDI is sent on channel 10. A row with no MIDI note number ignores both mouse buttons.

A strip along the bottom holds the controls. A Sync to menu, from 1 to 16 beats and defaulting to 4, sits beside Play. Without a LINK::Classes/Sequencer::, Play sets the playhead to beat 0 and waits for the next whole beat, or starts immediately if the clock is already on a beat. With a sequencer, Play tells the embedded LINK::Classes/RealTimePlayer:: to acquire that sequencer's shared clock. The clock is set to beat 0 when this play creates it. The playhead snaps onto the Sync-to grid, and playback starts from there immediately. The loop runs to the last step and then wraps. A second Play while already playing does nothing. While playing, the squares in the current column are outlined with LINK::Classes/GuiPalette#-extreme2::, unless that square is also in the selected row. Stop ends playback and clears that outline. Add step appends a column on the right of every row and lengthens the loop. If that column was removed earlier, its hits are shown again and play once the loop includes them. Remove step removes the rightmost column and is disabled when there are no steps. Hits in a removed column are kept. Removing the last step also stops playback. Add drum sound and Remove drum sound are hidden. Removing a row drops the hits for that drum sound.

It does not create a window. LINK::Classes/SequencerGui:: shows it from the Drums button. Place an instance in a LINK::Classes/Window:: layout to show it on its own.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
Creates an instance of TELETYPE::DrumProgrammer::.

ARGUMENT:: sequencer
An optional LINK::Classes/Sequencer::. When supplied, Play and Stop use that sequencer's shared clock. Pass CODE::nil:: to keep a standalone clock.

RETURNS::
A new TELETYPE::DrumProgrammer:: instance.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: stop
Stops playback and clears the playing-column outline. When this view was constructed with a LINK::Classes/Sequencer::, the embedded LINK::Classes/RealTimePlayer:: releases its own hold on that clock after its note-offs. Does nothing when playback is not in progress.

PRIVATE:: init, initClass, prAddDrumSound, prAddNote, prAddStep, prAppendDrumSound, prClearColumnHighlight, prClearRowSelection, prDropNotesForNoteNumber, prFindNote, prHighlightColumn, prInstallRow, prMakeButton, prMakeLabel, prMakeRow, prMakeStep, prNoteStartTime, prPlay, prRefreshRemoveButtons, prRemoveDrumSound, prRemoveNote, prRemoveStep, prSelectRow, prSelectedNotes, prSetButtonEnabled, prSortedDrumSoundNames, prStop, prSyncRowBackground, prSyncRowBorders, prSyncSequence, prSyncSquareBorder, prUpdateLoopEnd

EXAMPLES::

CODE::
(
var window = Window("Window", Rect(100, 100, 1000, 800));
window.layout = VLayout(DrumProgrammer()).margins_(0);
window.front;
)
::
